    slug: "rent-bidding-ban-landlords-2026", title: "Rent Bidding Ban: Rules for Landlords and Agents in 2026", description: "How the advertised asking rent, above-rent offers and pre-agreement payment rules changed in England from May 2026.", published: "2026-08-27", updated: "2026-08-27", readMins: 5,
    intro: "Since 1 May 2026, a landlord or agent advertising a rental property in England must state an asking rent and cannot encourage or accept bids above it. The rule changes how competitive applicant interest should be handled.",
    sections: [
      { h: "Publish an asking price", p: ["When a property is advertised, the asking rent must be published. The GOV.UK overview excludes a simple ‘to let’ sign from this advertising requirement.", "Use one clear amount across the listing and agent instructions. Ambiguous ‘offers over’ language is precisely the sort of nonsense the ban is intended to stop."] },
      { h: "Do not encourage higher offers", p: ["A landlord or agent must not invite, encourage or accept an offer above the advertised rent. That applies even where an applicant volunteers to pay more in an attempt to win the tenancy.", "Select tenants using lawful, consistent criteria rather than turning the application into an auction. Keep listing copies and decision records where several applications are received."] },
      { h: "Payments before signing", p: ["The Renters’ Rights overview also says landlords cannot ask for, encourage or accept rent before both landlord and tenant have signed the tenancy agreement.", "Deposits, holding deposits and permitted payments have their own rules. Do not rename rent or bundle charges to evade the process."] },
      { h: "Avoid discriminatory selection", p: ["The new letting rules prohibit discrimination against potential tenants because they receive benefits or have children. Application criteria should be relevant, consistent and documented.", "This does not require accepting every applicant. It requires a lawful process without blanket exclusions or illegal rent bidding."] },
    ], faqs: [{ q: "Can I accept more rent if the applicant offers it?", a: "No. The landlord or agent cannot accept an offer above the advertised asking rent." }, { q: "Must the asking rent be shown?", a: "Yes when advertising the property, subject to the specific exclusion for a simple ‘to let’ sign described by GOV.UK." }],
